---
title: Site kit
summary: A documentation-site kit on top of collections. Weave collections into a navigable site — nav tree, prev/next, search, sitemap, a shell — with the brains browser-safe and the corpus server-only.
related:
  - site/outline
  - site/shell
  - content/collections
---

A [collection](/docs/content/collections) answers **what exists** — a set of typed entries. The site kit answers **how they read as a site**: the nav tree, the order, the prev/next, the search index, the sitemap, the shell around the page. It is built directly on the content pillar and lives in the same `ogygia/content` barrel. **This site is one** — every page you are reading is woven, navigated, and searched by it.

You hand it collections and an arrangement; it mints the *brains* — small, data-returning, browser-safe functions the routes and the shell consume. The corpus itself never crosses the wire.

::: tip When to use
Reach for `defineSite()` when a collection needs to become a *site*: a sidebar, "keep reading", a search box, `llms.txt`, versioned or localized docs. If you only need a list and a body on one page, `content()` alone is enough — the site kit is the layer that turns a corpus into something you navigate.
:::

## From zero

Scaffold the whole thing — site module, content folder, routes, a `DocsShell` layout — in one command:

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npx ogygia site init
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm dlx ogygia site init
```

:::

The scaffold is ordinary app code you own from the first second: a `folder()` collection, a `defineSite()` next to it, a catch-all route, a layout mounting the shell. Everything below explains what those few files do.

## The whole thing in one file

`defineSite()` takes **one options bag** — `outline` is the only required key — and returns a `Site`. It lives in a **`.server.ts` module**, next to the collections it weaves:

```ts
// src/lib/site.server.ts — server-only: the corpus can never reach a client bundle
import { defineSite, links } from 'ogygia/content';
import { docs } from './collections.server';

export const site = defineSite({
	outline: docs,          // a bare collection weaves itself by convention (see below)
	prevNext: 'graph',      // "keep reading" follows the content graph
	checks: [links()],      // a broken in-prose link fails the build
	data: {
		title: 'Example',
		description: 'Docs for Example.',
		origin: 'https://example.dev'   // absolute URLs for sitemap/llms when prerendered
	}
});
```

That is the entire configuration for this site. `docs` is a `folder()` collection, so its `NN-` filename prefixes already encode order and its `+meta.json` sidecars name the sections — the outline reads that structure as **data** and weaves the tree with nothing more to say. Point it at several collections, or a `pick()` selection, or explicit groups when you want to arrange by hand — that is the [outline](/docs/site/outline).

::: warning Collections belong in a server module
A collection's loader is an *eager glob* — the whole compiled corpus. Define it in a `.server.ts` / `.remote.ts` file (or under `src/lib/server/`) and Kit mechanically prevents any client module from importing it, so the corpus can never leak into a browser bundle. Define one elsewhere and ogygia **warns** you at build time. See [Content collections](/docs/content/collections).
:::

## Brains, not a framework

`site` is a bag of read functions. Each returns plain data; none pulls the corpus into the browser:

| brain | returns | used by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `site.nav()` | the sidebar tree, hrefs resolved | the shell / a nav remote |
| `site.meta()` | `{ nav, switcher, data }` in one call | `<DocsShell {meta}>` |
| `site.doc(slug)` | one page's view — data, headings, body, prev/next | the page component |
| `site.search(q)` | ranked hits | a search remote / the worker |
| `site.switcher(slug)` | the version/locale picker (dimensioned sites) | `<Switcher>` |
| `site.emit.*` | GET handlers — sitemap, llms.txt, RSS, raw markdown, search index | `+server.ts` routes |
| `site.check()` | check findings, as data | vitest / CI |
| `site.entries()` | every leaf slug + declared old addresses | prerender `entries` |
| `site.load` | the 404 guard + alias 308s + per-page checks | the route's `load` |

Because they are data, **where** they run is your call. Prerender the nav, or fetch it per request; query search on the server, or ship the static index to a client worker. The [remotes](/docs/site/remotes) helper mints the common wire surface for you.

## The wire layer

The collection holds the compiled corpus — potentially megabytes of markdown. The site kit never serializes it. A page's **body** crosses the wire as a [held region](/docs/regions/held-regions) ticket (baked HTML, not source); everything else is metadata. [`remotes()`](/docs/site/remotes) mints the crossing from `site` in a `.remote.ts` — server-only, so the corpus stays server-side and the browser gets only the ticketed data:

```ts
// src/lib/site.remote.ts — the wire layer
import { remotes } from 'ogygia/content/server';
import { site } from './site.server';

export const { nav, meta, doc, search } = remotes(site, { base: '/docs' });
```

## Render it

The page component reads one brain and renders the view. The [`<Doc>`](/docs/site/shell) component lays out the article, the on-this-page rail, and prev/next from a `site.doc()` view; the [shell](/docs/site/shell) wraps it with the sidebar, header, and search:

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/docs/[...slug]/+page.svelte -->
<script>
	import { page } from '$app/state';
	import { Doc } from 'ogygia/content';
	import { doc } from '$lib/site.remote';

	const view = await doc(page.params.slug);
</script>

<Doc {view} />
```

And the layout mounts the shell over the `meta` remote, so the corpus never enters the layout's module graph:

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/docs/+layout.svelte -->
<script>
	import DocsShell from 'ogygia/content/docs-shell';
	import 'ogygia/content/theme.css';
	import 'ogygia/content/shell.css';
	import { meta } from '$lib/site.remote';
	let { children } = $props();

	const shellMeta = await meta();
</script>

<DocsShell meta={shellMeta} base="/docs" title="Docs">
	{@render children()}
</DocsShell>
```

## Request context: previews and roles

Every brain takes an optional **read context** — a plain object your `context` function derives from the request. Collections thread it into their `filter`, so one site serves a public projection and a preview projection from the same corpus:

```ts
export const site = defineSite({
	outline: docs,
	context: ({ cookies }) => ({ preview: cookies?.get?.('preview') === '1' })
});

// a draft page exists only in the preview projection
const publicView = await site.doc('roadmap');                        // null — filtered out
const draftView = await site.doc('roadmap', { context: { preview: true } });
```

The empty context `{}` is the public, prerendered projection. A context-gated site typically flips its [remotes](/docs/site/remotes) to `query` mode, so each request derives its own.

::: details The full options bag
Every `defineSite()` key, at a glance. `outline` is the only required one.

| key | what it does |
| --- | --- |
| `outline` | a collection, `outline([...])` spec, or `dimensions()` — the arrangement |
| `data` | `{ title, description, origin }` — site facts; emissions default from here |
| `base` | default mount prefix for every read; per-call `{ base }` overrides |
| `prevNext` | `'order'` (default) or `'graph'` — how "keep reading" is chosen |
| `trail` | `'weave'` (default) or `'group'` — whether prev/next crosses section boundaries |
| `checks` | `[links(), ...yours]` — corpus invariants; see [Checks](/docs/site/checks) |
| `components` | markdown element overrides, as real component values; see [Shell](/docs/site/shell) |
| `context` | derive `{ preview, roles, … }` from the request; threads into collection filters |
| `search` | `{ engine }` — swap the search engine; see [Search](/docs/site/search) |
| `redirects` | old addresses per entry (default reads `data.redirect_from`) — baked 308 stubs |
:::

## The map

The site kit is one surface with a few clear parts. Each has its own page:

- **[Outline](/docs/site/outline)** — weave collections into a tree. The spec grammar, filename conventions, the address map, prev/next.
- **[Dimensions](/docs/site/dimensions)** — one site across a matrix of versions or locales, with a switcher and graceful fallback.
- **[Shell & components](/docs/site/shell)** — `Frame` (headless), `DocsShell` and `BlogShell` (batteries-included), plus `Doc`, `Sidebar`, `Pager`, and the theme.
- **[Remotes](/docs/site/remotes)** — the wire layer: `nav`, `meta`, `doc`, `search`, prerendered or live.
- **[Search](/docs/site/search)** — the search brain, the static index, the on-device worker, the no-JS fallback.
- **[Emissions](/docs/site/emit)** — machine-facing serializations: sitemap, `llms.txt`, RSS, raw markdown.
- **[Checks](/docs/site/checks)** — the link audit and custom checks that fail the build, not the visitor.

## Do / don't

- **Do** keep `defineSite()` and its collections in a server module, and mint the wire in a `.remote.ts` — that split is what keeps the corpus out of client bundles.
- **Do** let filename conventions carry structure (`NN-` order, `+meta.json` labels). The outline reads them as data; you write no arrangement config for the common case.
- **Don't** pass a collection's bodies over the wire yourself. Let `site.doc()` bake the body into a region ticket — that is the only representation meant to cross.
- **Don't** reach for the site kit to render a single standalone page. That is `content()` plus `<Region>`; the site kit is the *site* around many.
